    "created_at": "2026-02-17T21:42:31.9227359Z",
    "response": " To find the work required to pump water from a cylindrical tank to a spout 1.5 meters above the top of the tank, we need to use the formula for work, which is given by:\n\n\\[ W = \\int_{a}^{b} F(x) \\, dx \\]\n\nwhere \\( F(x) \\) is the force required to lift a thin horizontal slice of water at height \\( x \\) and \\( dx \\) is the thickness of the slice. The force required to lift a slice of water is the product of its weight and the distance it must be lifted.\n\nFirst, let's determine the volume of a thin horizontal slice of water at height \\( x \\). The tank has a radius of 2 meters, so the area of the circular base is:\n\n\\[ A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2)^2 = 4\\pi \\, \\text{m}^2 \\]\n\nThe volume of a thin horizontal slice of water at height \\( x \\) with thickness \\( dx \\) is:\n\n\\[ dV = A \\, dx = 4\\pi \\, dx \\]\n\nThe mass of this slice of water is the product of its volume and the density of water:\n\n\\[ dm = \\rho \\, dV = 1000 \\cdot 4\\pi \\, dx = 4000\\pi \\, dx \\]\n\nThe weight of this slice of water is the product of its mass and the acceleration due to gravity:\n\n\\[ dF = dm \\cdot g = 4000\\pi \\, dx \\cdot 9.8 = 39200\\pi \\, dx \\]\n\nThe distance that this slice of water must be lifted is the distance from its height \\( x \\) to the spout, which is \\( (x + 1.5) \\",
